 ## Overview
-Two-stage k-means streamline clustering is a method for abstracting 3D vector fields by decomposing a large set of streamlines into coherent bundles and extracting representative streamlines for visualization [S1]. It solves the problem of visual clutter and high computational complexity associated with rendering raw streamline data by reducing the dataset to a concise set of representative paths [S1]. Unlike standard k-means which operates on high-dimensional point vectors, this method uses low-dimensional feature vectors (spatial coordinates and entropy metrics) to ensure efficiency and coherence in flow structure representation [S1].
+Two-stage k-means streamline clustering abstracts 3D vector fields by decomposing large streamline sets into coherent bundles and extracting representative paths for visualization [S1]. It solves visual clutter and high computational complexity by reducing raw streamline data to concise representative paths using low-dimensional feature vectors instead of full point clouds [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:** A set of generated streamlines from a 3D vector field [S1].
-**Outputs:** A set of representative streamlines, grouped into sub-bundles, suitable for illustrative rendering (e.g., as streamtapes) [S1].
+**Outputs:** A set of representative streamlines grouped into sub-bundles, ready for illustrative rendering (e.g., as streamtapes) [S1].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-Each streamline is represented by a 1D feature vector [S1]. The method distinguishes between two types of feature vectors used in different stages:
-1. **Spatial Property Vector:** A 9-component vector comprising the start point, middle point, and end point of the streamline [S1].
-2. **Shape Property Vector:** A 2-component vector comprising Linear Entropy ($E_L$) and Angular Entropy ($E_A$) [S1].
